            <history xml:id="history_333N200F6">
               <eventList type="performances" xml:id="eventList_9ed2807c">
                  <event xml:id="event_fc0ed616">
                     <date isodate="1890-12-11" xml:id="date_ad97793e">1890-12-11</date>
                     <geogName role="place" xml:id="geogName_074205a8">Berlin</geogName>
                     <persName role="" xml:id="persName_0d268037">Ludwig Mendelssohn, piano</persName>
                     <desc xml:id="idm94">Concert in Freie musikalische Vereinigung.</desc>
                     <biblList xml:id="biblList_c44f600a">
                        <head xml:id="head_05e8701a">Reviews</head>
                     </biblList>
                     <biblList xml:id="biblList_c1030d07">
                        <head xml:id="head_a75a3584">Evidence</head>
                     </biblList>
                  </event>
               </eventList>
               <p xml:id="idm99">Hartmann was apparently in doubt about what to call this collection, for on 5 December 1851 he referred to them in a letter to his publisher O.H. Delbanco as "mine 6 navnløse Børn" (My 6 nameless children); however, four weeks later he called them "Caprices instructives". When they were published shortly aftewards, he had decided to call them "Etudes Instructives".</p>
            </history>
